{'question_id': 'qst_0404', 'context_precision': 0.9999999999, 'context_recall': 0.14285714285714285, 'question': 'We saw quality regressions after enabling an INT8/mixed-precision variant and suspect KV-cache rounding on long-context requests. What request header can we set as a quick mitigation to bypass the KV cache, and in the dynamic quant handoff policy for the interactive-chat route what minimum KV-cache hit-rate and max sequence length are required before routing to a quant variant?', 'response': 'The request header is `X-RW-Cache-Mode: bypass` (enable prefix-KV bypass for long-context sessions, per-route).\n\nFor the dynamic quant handoff policy for the interactive-chat route, the context does not provide the minimum KV-cache hit-rate or max sequence length thresholds. 未找到相关文档。', 'gold_answer': 'Set the request header X-RW-Cache-Mode: bypass to bypass the KV cache. For the interactive-chat dynamic quant handoff policy, only route to a quant variant when KV-cache hit-rate is at least 60% and the request sequence length is at most 2048 tokens.', 'reference': "Setting the request header X-RW-Cache-Mode: bypass bypasses the KV cache.\nFor the interactive-chat dynamic quant handoff policy, routing to a quant variant requires a KV-cache hit-rate of at least 60%.\nFor the interactive-chat dynamic quant handoff policy, routing to a quant variant requires the request sequence length to be at most 2048 tokens.\nThe answer must not use a different cache-bypass header name (e.g., anything other than X-RW-Cache-Mode: bypass).\nThe answer must not claim the interactive-chat route's minimum KV-cache hit-rate is 30% or 0% (those appear for other routes in the routing matrix).\nThe answer must not claim the interactive-chat route's max sequence length for quant routing is 8192 or 1024 (those limits belong to other routes in the routing matrix).\nThe answer must not substitute general quantization rollback/eval thresholds (RSI, fidelity deltas, etc.) in place of the routing requirements requested.", 'retrieved_contexts': ['numeric divergence (attention scaling, softmax underflow).KV-cache misalignment (seen on long-context prompts; tokens shift after cache boundary).Quantization-induced top-token rank flips (semantic change).Serving routing variant (different model compiled kernel used).Quick mitigations (apply in parallel if safe):Route affected traffic to float/less-quantized profile (canary 5–10%).Enable prefix-KV bypass for long-context sessions: add headerX-RW-Cache-Mode: bypassper-route.Pin a previous model_version for the affected route and initiate graceful rollback.Verify mitigations with smoke evals and customer probesRun the same sample through mitigated route for N=500 requests and confirm RSI delta returns below 0.02.Verification checklist and artifacts to collect:triage.csv with per-request metrics (RSI, embedding_cosine, top-k-KL).compiled kernel metadata:runtime-inspect --model <id> --kernel-matrixquant profile JSON used (attach as artifact). Example snippet:quant_profile: quant-a', 'Quantized output degradation: detection and repair protocolSummary:This playbook describes a pragmatic, repeatable protocol for detecting, validating, and repairing service-impacting output degradations that are introduced or amplified by quantized model deployments. It targets engineers operating Redwood serving lanes (hosted, dedicated, private) and the applied ML teams responsible for model promotion and quant profiles.Scope and goals:Detect degradations that are plausibly caused by quantization or quant+KV-cache interactions.Provide deterministic triage steps that reduce time-to-root-cause to < 8 hours for high-severity incidents.Present automated verification checks and a minimal repair checklist to restore behavior while preserving cost benefits.Recommend long-term fixes (profile adjustments, per-workload calibration, shadow-eval gating).Why this matters:Quantization reduces unit cost but introduces subtle discontinuities in model outputs (token preferences, beam-path collapse,'], 'sources': ['dsid_ad70fa7335294e2988193e2a6645a5ba__quant-output-degradation-detect-and-repair-protocol-2026.txt']}
{'question_id': 'qst_0327', 'context_precision': 0, 'context_recall': 0, 'question': 'In the Unified Inference Consumption Paradigm overview, what are the two unit primitives used for billing, and where is the referenced Confluence file that contains the pricing catalog and token definitions for them?', 'response': '未找到相关文档。', 'gold_answer': 'The two billing unit primitives are the token-generation-unit (TGU) and the embedding-unit (EU). The pricing catalog and token definitions for them are referenced in the Confluence file at "/confluence/product-docs/pricing-and-packaging/token-bundle-flex-enterprise-metering-2026-08-20.json".', 'reference': 'The two billing unit primitives are the token-generation-unit (TGU) and the embedding-unit (EU).\nThe pricing catalog and token definitions for these units are referenced in the Confluence file at /confluence/product-docs/pricing-and-packaging/token-bundle-flex-enterprise-metering-2026-08-20.json.', 'retrieved_contexts': [], 'sources': []}
{'question_id': 'qst_0110', 'context_precision': 0.0, 'context_recall': 0.0, 'question': 'What is the rollout ramp schedule and time window used for the canary phase when validating adaptive degradation behavior in the Redwood serving runtime?', 'response': '未找到相关文档。', 'gold_answer': 'The canary phase ramps traffic from 1% to 5% to 20% in 30-minute windows, with automated health checks run every 5 minutes.', 'reference': 'The canary phase ramps traffic from 1% to 5% to 20%.\nEach ramp step uses a 30-minute time window.', 'retrieved_contexts': ['Adaptive Degradation Safety & Verification ManualSummary:\\n\\nThis manual defines the safety contracts, verification checks, benchmarking protocols, and operational runbooks we use to validate adaptive degradation and fallback behavior in the Redwood serving runtime. It is intended for runtime engineers, SREs, and release owners who gate runtime and scheduler changes. The document consolidates architecture contracts, test harnesses, profiling playbooks, measurable acceptance criteria, and incident response steps into a single reference.\\n\\nScope:\\n- Covers adaptive degradation (fidelity staircases, model-variant fallback, burst shedding) and scheduler/kernel interactions.\\n- Includes verification checks executed in CI, pre-release staging, and canary phases.\\n- Prescribes profiling and benchmark procedures for SLO verification and performance regressions.\\n- Provides runbooks for on-call and release owners for graceful rollback and mitigation.\\n\\nPrinciples and safety goals:\\n1)', 'outputs.\\n4) Upload artifacts to /artifacts/regressions/.\\n\\nRelated reading and references:\\n- See routing and fallback architecture ADR (link in related_pages).\\n- Profiling playbooks and telemetry contract (links in related_pages).\\n\\nNotes and change log:\\n- 2026-01-15: Initial draft and adoption of verification matrix (S. Rao).\\n- 2026-03-10: Added canary ramp policy and refined kernel warmup contract; added runbook remediation steps (E. Hart, D. Alvarez).\\n\\nIf you are implementing a new scheduler or kernel change, follow this manual start-to-finish: 1) add microbench checks, 2) register threshold baselines, 3) run staging replay, 4) validate VD-* checks, 5) enable canary with the configured ramp, 6) monitor and be ready to rollback.\\n'], 'sources': ['dsid_2e4183d040b341cf843366e2c15402af__adaptive-degradation-safety-verification-manual-2026.txt']}
{'question_id': 'qst_0435', 'context_precision': 0.0, 'context_recall': 1.0, 'question': "How many weekly status updates for Project Orion marked the project as 'At Risk'?", 'response': '3', 'gold_answer': 'Three weekly status updates for Project Orion marked the project as "At Risk" (2026-01-16, 2026-01-23, and 2026-02-06).', 'reference': 'Three weekly status updates for Project Orion were marked as At Risk\nThe At Risk weekly status updates occurred on 2026-01-16, 2026-01-23, and 2026-02-06', 'retrieved_contexts': ['Project Orion — Weekly status update (2026-01-16)SummaryOrion is progressing, but we have an emerging integration risk with the runtime router path for fallback decisions.StatusOverall: At RiskRAG: RedProgress this weekPolicy engine skeleton merged behind a feature flag (orion_policy_engine_enabled).Defined fallback precedence rules for model variants (pinning > compatibility > cost).Risks / blockersRuntime router currently applies fallback in a different code path than the control plane expects; we may need a refactor or a shim layer.Timeline risk: if refactor is required, console beta slips by 1–2 weeks.MitigationsSchedule a 90-minute design review with runtime + platform owners.Implement a temporary adapter that maps router decisions into Orion policy evaluation events.Next weekPrototype adapter in staging and validate event schema.OwnersPM: Ava ChenEng: Alex Martinez (platform), Connor OBrien (runtime routing), Maya Srinivasan (console)', 'Project Orion — Weekly status update (2026-01-23)SummaryPrimary risk remains the runtime routing integration. We also uncovered a requirement gap around auditability of fallback decisions for enterprise customers.StatusOverall: At RiskRAG: RedProgress this weekCompleted design review: agreed to keep router decisioning local for latency, but emit standardized decision events.Drafted event schema for policy evaluation and fallback decision logs.Risks / blockersRuntime team concern: additional per-request overhead for emitting decision events.Enterprise requirement: Dedicated customers want an auditable trail of why fallback occurred (capacity vs policy vs compatibility).MitigationsEmit decision events asynchronously and sample at 1% by default; allow customer-tier overrides.Add a "fallback_reason" enum and store only minimal fields required for audit logging.Next weekImplement event emission in runtime router behind a gate; validate overhead in perf canary.OwnersPM: Ava ChenEng: Connor', 'Project Orion — Weekly status update (2026-02-06)SummaryWe hit a console delivery risk: validation and policy preview work is larger than planned, and the beta cohort onboarding may slip.StatusOverall: At RiskRAG: YellowProgress this weekConsole policy editor: added compatibility validation for model variants (client-side).Added basic policy preview panel showing expected canary split and fallback precedence.Risks / blockersPolicy preview requires backend simulation endpoints; current implementation is static and may mislead users.Beta cohort (3 design partners) is waiting on a working preview to sign off.MitigationsScope preview to a read-only backend call for a single route and a single region first.Provide a temporary “preview is approximate” disclaimer until simulation is complete.Next weekAdd control plane simulation endpoint (simulate_policy_v1).Run a beta walkthrough with the solutions engineering team.OwnersPM: Ava ChenEng: Maya Srinivasan (console), Alex Martinez (control', "Project Orion — Weekly status update (2026-01-30)SummaryWe reduced the main integration risk by landing a low-overhead decision-event emission path and validating its performance impact.StatusOverall: On TrackRAG: GreenProgress this weekRuntime router: implemented asynchronous decision event emission behind a gate (orion_decision_events_enabled).Perf: measured overhead at p95 < 0.3ms for chat workloads with 1% sampling.Control plane: implemented a basic policy evaluation endpoint returning canonical fallback_reason values.Risks / blockersConsole policy editor still missing validation rules for incompatible model variants.Next weekAdd compatibility validation in console (block save if variant is not compatible).Start end-to-end integration test: console -> control plane -> router -> decision event viewer.OwnersPM: Ava ChenEng: Connor O'Brien (runtime), Alex Martinez (control plane), Hiro Tanaka (perf), Maya Srinivasan (console)", 'Project Orion — Weekly status update (2026-01-09)SummaryProject Orion is the cross-team effort to unify rollout controls (canary, A/B, and automatic fallback) into a single policy surface in the Redwood Console and routing layer.StatusOverall: On TrackRAG: GreenProgress this weekLanded initial API contract for policy evaluation (route selector -> policy engine).Console wireframes approved for the policy editor v1.Defined success criteria for v0 (single-model canary + fallback).Risks / blockersNone material.DecisionsOrion v0 will support canary by percentage and automatic rollback on 5xx rate regression.Next weekImplement policy engine skeleton in platform-control-plane.Start integration with runtime router (read-only evaluation).OwnersPM: Ava ChenEng: Alex Martinez (platform), Maya Srinivasan (console)'], 'sources': ['dsid_a134a3fd3c244a52a8d3e1ff515fdf58__project-orion-weekly-update-2026-01-16.txt', 'dsid_ce60931a1aea428b8186228ee40bb497__project-orion-weekly-update-2026-01-23.txt', 'dsid_431b0713c42847bbac15f3354776db68__project-orion-weekly-update-2026-02-06.txt', 'dsid_03e22d519f9b40a5ac61a03e5c4620d3__project-orion-weekly-update-2026-01-30.txt', 'dsid_7fc490f8522b486c8f4b665d5189066c__project-orion-weekly-update-2026-01-09.txt']}